============================================================
       CHAPTER 13 — DYNAMIC MEMORY ALLOCATION IN C
       Complete Deep-Dive | Job-Ready | Interview Ready
============================================================

Dynamic Memory Allocation is one of the most important topics
after pointers.

It allows a C program to request memory at runtime instead of
deciding the complete memory requirement at compile time.

This chapter connects:

    POINTERS
        +
    MEMORY
        +
    malloc/calloc/realloc/free
        +
    DATA STRUCTURES
        +
    MEMORY SAFETY

------------------------------------------------------------
1. WHAT IS DYNAMIC MEMORY ALLOCATION?
------------------------------------------------------------

Dynamic Memory Allocation (DMA) means allocating and releasing
memory during program execution.

Example:

    int *p = malloc(10 * sizeof *p);

The program decides at runtime that it needs space for
10 integers.

This is different from:

    int arr[10];

where the array size is fixed for that declaration.

Dynamic allocation is useful when:

    • Size is known only at runtime
    • Data size changes during execution
    • Large data structures are required
    • Linked lists are required
    • Trees/graphs are required
    • Dynamic strings are required
    • A program needs flexible memory usage

------------------------------------------------------------
2. STATIC / AUTOMATIC / DYNAMIC STORAGE
------------------------------------------------------------

A simplified view of common C storage durations:

    Storage duration
          |
          +------------------+
          |                  |
       Automatic          Static
          |
       local variables

Dynamic allocated objects have allocated storage duration and
remain allocated until released with free (or until program
termination, depending on the implementation/environment).

Example:

    void test(void)
    {
        int x = 10;              // automatic storage duration
        static int y = 20;       // static storage duration
    }

Dynamic:

    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

The allocated object exists until:

    free(p);

------------------------------------------------------------
3. STACK VS HEAP
------------------------------------------------------------

A common conceptual model:

    STACK
    --------------------
    local automatic data
    function call data
    --------------------

    HEAP / ALLOCATED STORAGE
    ------------------------
    dynamically allocated objects
    ------------------------

IMPORTANT:

"Stack" and "heap" are common implementation concepts, but the
C standard describes storage duration and allocation semantics
rather than requiring a particular physical memory layout.

For interviews, you should understand the practical distinction.

Example:

    void function(void)
    {
        int x = 10;

        int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

        if (p != NULL)
        {
            *p = 20;
            free(p);
        }
    }

Here:

    x -> automatic storage duration

    *p -> dynamically allocated object

------------------------------------------------------------
4. WHY DYNAMIC MEMORY?
------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose the user enters the number of students:

    Enter number of students: 5000

You don't necessarily want:

    Student students[100000];

Instead:

    size_t n;

    scanf("%zu", &n);

    Student *students = malloc(n * sizeof *students);

Now the required amount can be determined at runtime.

------------------------------------------------------------
5. malloc()
------------------------------------------------------------

Header:

    #include <stdlib.h>

Syntax:

    malloc(number_of_bytes)

Example:

    int *p = malloc(5 * sizeof *p);

This requests enough storage for 5 int objects.

malloc returns:

    void *

which can be assigned to an object pointer in C without an
explicit cast.

GOOD:

    int *p = malloc(5 * sizeof *p);

Generally avoid:

    int *p = (int *)malloc(5 * sizeof *p);

The cast is unnecessary in C and can hide a missing declaration
of malloc if the proper header is not included.

------------------------------------------------------------
6. malloc() MEMORY IS NOT INITIALIZED
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int *p = malloc(5 * sizeof *p);

Do NOT assume:

    p[0] == 0
    p[1] == 0

The allocated bytes have indeterminate values until you
initialize the objects appropriately.

Example:

    for (size_t i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        p[i] = 0;
    }

------------------------------------------------------------
7. ALWAYS CHECK malloc()
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int *p = malloc(100 * sizeof *p);

    if (p == NULL)
    {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
        return 1;
    }

Only use p after successful allocation.

------------------------------------------------------------
8. BASIC malloc PROGRAM
------------------------------------------------------------

    #include <stdio.h>
    #include <stdlib.h>

    int main(void)
    {
        int *p = malloc(5 * sizeof *p);

        if (p == NULL)
        {
            return 1;
        }

        for (size_t i = 0; i < 5; i++)
        {
            p[i] = (int)(i + 1) * 10;
        }

        for (size_t i = 0; i < 5; i++)
        {
            printf("%d ", p[i]);
        }

        printf("\n");

        free(p);
        p = NULL;

        return 0;
    }

Output:

    10 20 30 40 50

------------------------------------------------------------
9. sizeof WITH malloc()
------------------------------------------------------------

Preferred:

    int *p = malloc(10 * sizeof *p);

Why?

Because if the type changes:

    double *p

then:

    malloc(10 * sizeof *p)

automatically uses the correct element size.

Less maintainable:

    malloc(10 * sizeof(int))

Both can be correct, but sizeof *p is often safer against
type changes.

------------------------------------------------------------
10. ALLOCATION SIZE CALCULATION
------------------------------------------------------------

For N elements:

    N * sizeof(type)

Example:

    double *arr = malloc(n * sizeof *arr);

This means:

    n objects × size of one object

------------------------------------------------------------
11. INTEGER OVERFLOW IN SIZE CALCULATIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

This is an important security issue.

Potentially dangerous:

    int n = ...;

    malloc(n * sizeof *p);

If the multiplication overflows before being passed to malloc,
you may allocate less memory than expected and later overflow it.

Use an appropriate unsigned size type such as:

    size_t

and validate the requested size.

For example:

    if (n > SIZE_MAX / sizeof *p)
    {
        /* allocation size would overflow */
    }

You may need:

    #include <stdint.h>

for SIZE_MAX on implementations that provide it there.

------------------------------------------------------------
12. calloc()
------------------------------------------------------------

Syntax:

    calloc(number_of_elements, element_size)

Example:

    int *p = calloc(5, sizeof *p);

calloc allocates storage for 5 int objects and initializes the
allocated bytes to all-bits-zero.

Example:

    int *p = calloc(5, sizeof *p);

Then for integer objects, the resulting values are normally
zero on conventional implementations.

Important technical point:

calloc guarantees all allocated bytes are initialized to zero.
Do not turn this into the overly broad claim that every possible
C type has a zero value represented by all-bits-zero.

------------------------------------------------------------
13. malloc() VS calloc()
------------------------------------------------------------

                malloc                 calloc
------------------------------------------------------------
Arguments       1                      2
                bytes                  count + size

Initialization  No initialization     All bytes zeroed

Example:

    malloc(n * sizeof *p)

    calloc(n, sizeof *p)

Use calloc when you specifically want zeroed allocated storage.

------------------------------------------------------------
14. calloc EXAMPLE
------------------------------------------------------------

    #include <stdio.h>
    #include <stdlib.h>

    int main(void)
    {
        size_t n = 5;

        int *arr = calloc(n, sizeof *arr);

        if (arr == NULL)
        {
            return 1;
        }

        for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            printf("%d ", arr[i]);
        }

        printf("\n");

        free(arr);

        return 0;
    }

Typical output:

    0 0 0 0 0

------------------------------------------------------------
15. realloc()
------------------------------------------------------------

realloc changes the size of an existing allocated object.

Syntax:

    realloc(pointer, new_size)

Example:

    int *p = malloc(5 * sizeof *p);

    int *temp = realloc(p, 10 * sizeof *p);

If successful:

    temp

points to the resized allocation.

------------------------------------------------------------
16. IMPORTANT realloc() RULE
------------------------------------------------------------

Do NOT normally write:

    p = realloc(p, new_size);

if you need to keep p valid when realloc fails.

Why?

If realloc returns NULL because it cannot allocate the requested
size, the original allocation is still valid.

But if you overwrite p:

    p = NULL;

you lose the original pointer and leak the original allocation.

Better:

    int *temp = realloc(p, new_size);

    if (temp != NULL)
    {
        p = temp;
    }
    else
    {
        /* p is still valid */
    }

------------------------------------------------------------
17. realloc() EXAMPLE
------------------------------------------------------------

    #include <stdio.h>
    #include <stdlib.h>

    int main(void)
    {
        size_t n = 5;

        int *arr = malloc(n * sizeof *arr);

        if (arr == NULL)
        {
            return 1;
        }

        for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            arr[i] = (int)(i + 1);
        }

        size_t new_n = 10;

        int *temp = realloc(arr, new_n * sizeof *arr);

        if (temp == NULL)
        {
            free(arr);
            return 1;
        }

        arr = temp;

        for (size_t i = n; i < new_n; i++)
        {
            arr[i] = 0;
        }

        for (size_t i = 0; i < new_n; i++)
        {
            printf("%d ", arr[i]);
        }

        printf("\n");

        free(arr);

        return 0;
    }

------------------------------------------------------------
18. realloc() CAN MOVE MEMORY
------------------------------------------------------------

Important:

    realloc()

may:

    • Expand the existing block in place

OR:

    • Allocate a new block
    • Copy the old contents
    • Release the old block
    • Return the new address

Therefore:

    int *old = p;

After realloc:

    p

may have a different address.

Any pointer into the old allocation may become invalid if
realloc moves the allocation.

------------------------------------------------------------
19. POINTERS INTO realloc() MEMORY
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int *p = malloc(5 * sizeof *p);

    int *q = &p[2];

    int *temp = realloc(p, 10 * sizeof *p);

If realloc moves the allocation, q is no longer valid.

Therefore, after realloc, pointers into the old block should be
recomputed from the new base pointer if needed.

------------------------------------------------------------
20. SHRINKING WITH realloc()
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int *p = malloc(10 * sizeof *p);

    int *temp = realloc(p, 5 * sizeof *p);

If successful:

    p = temp;

Now the allocation is large enough for 5 int objects.

Data beyond the new size is no longer part of the allocation.

Never access:

    p[5]
    p[6]
    ...

after shrinking to 5 elements.

------------------------------------------------------------
21. free()
------------------------------------------------------------

Syntax:

    free(pointer);

Example:

    int *p = malloc(10 * sizeof *p);

    if (p != NULL)
    {
        free(p);
        p = NULL;
    }

free releases the allocated object.

------------------------------------------------------------
22. free(NULL)
------------------------------------------------------------

This is safe:

    int *p = NULL;

    free(p);

No action is performed.

Therefore:

    free(p);
    p = NULL;

is a useful cleanup pattern.

------------------------------------------------------------
23. MEMORY LEAK
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int *p = malloc(100 * sizeof *p);

    p = NULL;

The allocated object is now unreachable.

This is a memory leak.

Correct:

    int *p = malloc(100 * sizeof *p);

    if (p != NULL)
    {
        /* use memory */

        free(p);
        p = NULL;
    }

------------------------------------------------------------
24. DANGLING POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

    if (p != NULL)
    {
        *p = 100;

        free(p);

        /* p still contains the old address */
    }

After free:

    p

is a dangling pointer unless you stop using it.

Safer:

    free(p);
    p = NULL;

------------------------------------------------------------
25. USE-AFTER-FREE
------------------------------------------------------------

BAD:

    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

    if (p != NULL)
    {
        *p = 10;

        free(p);

        printf("%d\n", *p);
    }

This accesses an object whose lifetime has ended.

Result:

    Undefined behavior

------------------------------------------------------------
26. DOUBLE FREE
------------------------------------------------------------

BAD:

    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

    free(p);
    free(p);

The second free is invalid.

Safer:

    free(p);
    p = NULL;

------------------------------------------------------------
27. WILD POINTER
------------------------------------------------------------

BAD:

    int *p;

    *p = 10;

p is uninitialized.

Correct:

    int x;
    int *p = &x;

or:

    int *p = NULL;

------------------------------------------------------------
28. ALLOCATING A SINGLE OBJECT
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

    if (p == NULL)
    {
        return 1;
    }

    *p = 100;

    printf("%d\n", *p);

    free(p);

------------------------------------------------------------
29. ALLOCATING STRUCTURES
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    struct Student
    {
        int age;
        float marks;
    };

Dynamic allocation:

    struct Student *s =
        malloc(sizeof *s);

Check:

    if (s == NULL)
    {
        return 1;
    }

Initialize:

    s->age = 22;
    s->marks = 90.5f;

Use:

    printf("%d %.2f\n",
           s->age,
           s->marks);

Release:

    free(s);

------------------------------------------------------------
30. DYNAMIC ARRAY OF STRUCTURES
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    struct Student
    {
        int age;
        float marks;
    };

    size_t n = 100;

    struct Student *students =
        malloc(n * sizeof *students);

Now:

    students[0]
    students[1]
    ...
    students[n - 1]

can be used.

------------------------------------------------------------
31. DYNAMIC STRING
------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose the required length is known:

    size_t length = 20;

Allocate:

    char *str = malloc((length + 1) * sizeof *str);

Why +1?

For:

    '\0'

Example:

    char *str = malloc(21);

Then at most 20 characters can be stored plus:

    '\0'

------------------------------------------------------------
32. DYNAMIC STRING EXAMPLE
------------------------------------------------------------

    #include <stdio.h>
    #include <stdlib.h>
    #include <string.h>

    int main(void)
    {
        const char *source = "Apoorv";

        size_t len = strlen(source);

        char *copy = malloc(len + 1);

        if (copy == NULL)
        {
            return 1;
        }

        memcpy(copy, source, len + 1);

        printf("%s\n", copy);

        free(copy);

        return 0;
    }

Output:

    Apoorv

------------------------------------------------------------
33. WHY len + 1?
------------------------------------------------------------

For:

    "Hello"

Memory contains:

    H
    e
    l
    l
    o
    \0

strlen gives:

    5

Required capacity:

    6

Therefore:

    strlen(str) + 1

------------------------------------------------------------
34. DYNAMIC 2D ARRAY — METHOD 1
------------------------------------------------------------

You can allocate a contiguous 2D array.

Example:

    size_t rows = 3;
    size_t cols = 4;

    int *matrix =
        malloc(rows * cols * sizeof *matrix);

Access:

    matrix[i * cols + j]

Example:

    matrix[1 * cols + 2]

represents:

    row 1, column 2

This creates one contiguous allocation.

------------------------------------------------------------
35. DYNAMIC 2D ARRAY — METHOD 2
------------------------------------------------------------

Pointer-to-pointer style:

    int **matrix = malloc(rows * sizeof *matrix);

Then allocate each row separately:

    for (size_t i = 0; i < rows; i++)
    {
        matrix[i] = malloc(cols * sizeof *matrix[i]);
    }

Now:

    matrix[i][j]

can be used.

But cleanup must be done row by row:

    for (size_t i = 0; i < rows; i++)
    {
        free(matrix[i]);
    }

    free(matrix);

This is NOT the same memory layout as one contiguous
rows × cols allocation.

------------------------------------------------------------
36. DYNAMIC 2D ARRAY — METHOD 3
------------------------------------------------------------

A pointer to an array can provide convenient indexing while using
one contiguous allocation.

For a runtime column count, one possible form is:

    size_t rows = 3;
    size_t cols = 4;

    int (*matrix)[cols] =
        malloc(rows * sizeof *matrix);

Then:

    matrix[i][j]

can be used.

This uses a variable length array type in the pointer-to-array
declaration. Support and constraints depend on the C version and
implementation, so understand this after mastering the simpler
forms.

------------------------------------------------------------
37. FLEXIBLE ARRAY MEMBER
------------------------------------------------------------

C supports flexible array members in structures.

Example:

    struct Packet
    {
        size_t length;
        unsigned char data[];
    };

A flexible array member can be used when a structure and its
variable-length trailing data are allocated together.

Conceptually:

    +------------------+
    | length           |
    +------------------+
    | data             |
    | data             |
    | data             |
    +------------------+

This is an advanced but important systems-programming technique.

------------------------------------------------------------
38. OWNERSHIP
------------------------------------------------------------

Dynamic memory requires clear ownership.

Ask:

    Who allocated it?

    Who owns it?

    Who can modify it?

    Who frees it?

    How long is it valid?

Example:

    int *create_array(size_t n)
    {
        return malloc(n * sizeof(int));
    }

If the caller receives the returned pointer, the API should make
it clear that the caller is responsible for:

    free()

when the memory is no longer required.

------------------------------------------------------------
39. TRANSFERRING OWNERSHIP
------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose:

    int *p = malloc(100 * sizeof *p);

A function can take ownership:

    void consume(int *p)
    {
        /* use p */
        free(p);
    }

Then the caller must not use or free p again after ownership has
been transferred.

Good software design makes ownership rules explicit.

------------------------------------------------------------
40. ALLOCATION FAILURE
------------------------------------------------------------

Allocation can fail.

Example:

    int *p = malloc(n * sizeof *p);

    if (p == NULL)
    {
        /* handle failure */
    }

Do not write:

    *p = 10;

before checking p if failure is possible.

In production software, the response to allocation failure depends
on the application:

    • Return an error
    • Clean up
    • Retry smaller allocation
    • Abort
    • Use fallback storage

------------------------------------------------------------
41. MEMORY FRAGMENTATION
------------------------------------------------------------

Repeated allocation and freeing can create fragmented free space.

Example:

    allocate A
    allocate B
    allocate C

    free B

Now there may be a gap between A and C.

The exact behavior depends on the allocator.

Fragmentation can affect:

    • Memory utilization
    • Allocation performance
    • Long-running applications

This matters particularly in:

    embedded systems
    servers
    operating systems
    real-time systems

------------------------------------------------------------
42. malloc(0)
------------------------------------------------------------

Code such as:

    malloc(0)

has implementation-defined allocation behavior.

It may return NULL or a unique pointer value that can be passed
to free.

Do not assume it always behaves like a normal nonempty allocation.

If your program needs at least one element, validate the count
before allocating.

------------------------------------------------------------
43. realloc(NULL, size)
------------------------------------------------------------

Important rule:

    realloc(NULL, size)

behaves like:

    malloc(size)

for the allocation semantics.

This can sometimes simplify dynamic-array implementations.

------------------------------------------------------------
44. realloc(ptr, 0)
------------------------------------------------------------

Do not build portable modern C code around assuming one particular
result from realloc(ptr, 0).

The historical and standard-version details are subtle.

For clear code, when you simply want to release an allocation:

    free(ptr);
    ptr = NULL;

is preferable.

------------------------------------------------------------
45. SAFE ALLOCATION PATTERN
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    #include <stdlib.h>

    int *create_array(size_t n)
    {
        if (n > SIZE_MAX / sizeof(int))
        {
            return NULL;
        }

        return malloc(n * sizeof(int));
    }

Depending on the implementation/environment, include the header
providing SIZE_MAX.

A simpler common pattern is:

    int *p = malloc(n * sizeof *p);

but robust code should consider integer overflow when n can be
untrusted or extremely large.

------------------------------------------------------------
46. REALLOC SAFE PATTERN
------------------------------------------------------------

Use:

    int *temp = realloc(p, new_size);

    if (temp == NULL)
    {
        /* original p remains valid */
    }
    else
    {
        p = temp;
    }

This pattern is essential for professional C programming.

------------------------------------------------------------
47. DYNAMIC ARRAY GROWTH
------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose capacity is:

    4

When full:

    capacity = 8

Then:

    16
    32
    64
    ...

This reduces the number of reallocations.

Typical dynamic-array concept:

    size
    capacity
    data pointer

Example:

    struct Vector
    {
        int *data;
        size_t size;
        size_t capacity;
    };

This is the foundation of vector-like data structures.

------------------------------------------------------------
48. DYNAMIC ARRAY STRUCTURE
------------------------------------------------------------

Concept:

    struct Vector
    {
        int *data;
        size_t size;
        size_t capacity;
    };

Meaning:

    data
        allocated memory

    size
        number of actual elements

    capacity
        number of elements that can fit before resizing

Example:

    size = 3
    capacity = 8

Memory:

    [10][20][30][ ][ ][ ][ ][ ]

Only the first 3 are currently elements.

------------------------------------------------------------
49. MEMORY INITIALIZATION
------------------------------------------------------------

malloc:

    memory contents are not initialized.

calloc:

    allocated bytes are zeroed.

You can explicitly initialize:

    memset(p, 0, n * sizeof *p);

But be careful:

    memset(p, 1, n * sizeof *p);

does NOT generally mean "set every integer to 1".

It sets every BYTE to 0x01.

For an int array, this commonly produces:

    0x01010101

rather than integer value 1.

------------------------------------------------------------
50. memset()
------------------------------------------------------------

Header:

    #include <string.h>

Example:

    char buffer[100];

    memset(buffer, 0, sizeof buffer);

This sets every byte to zero.

For raw byte buffers, this is very useful.

For general typed objects, understand their representation before
using memset as a value-initialization technique.

------------------------------------------------------------
51. memcpy() WITH DYNAMIC MEMORY
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int source[] = {1, 2, 3};

    int *destination =
        malloc(sizeof source);

    if (destination != NULL)
    {
        memcpy(destination,
               source,
               sizeof source);

        free(destination);
    }

The destination must have sufficient storage.

------------------------------------------------------------
52. memmove() WITH DYNAMIC MEMORY
------------------------------------------------------------

If source and destination regions overlap:

    memmove()

must be used rather than:

    memcpy()

Example:

    memmove(arr + 1,
            arr,
            n * sizeof *arr);

This can safely move overlapping memory ranges.

------------------------------------------------------------
53. POINTER AFTER free()
------------------------------------------------------------

Important:

    free(p);

does not modify p itself.

Therefore:

    p != NULL

may still be true after free.

This is why:

    free(p);
    p = NULL;

is a useful pattern when p remains in scope.

------------------------------------------------------------
54. MULTIPLE POINTERS TO ONE ALLOCATION
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

    int *q = p;

Now:

    p
    |
    +----> allocated object
    |
    q
    +---->

If you do:

    free(p);

then q is also dangling.

Setting:

    p = NULL;

does NOT make q safe.

This is why ownership and aliasing matter.

------------------------------------------------------------
55. MEMORY LEAK THROUGH ALIASING
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int *p = malloc(100 * sizeof *p);

    int *q = p;

    free(p);

    p = NULL;

q still contains the old address.

Using q is invalid.

This demonstrates why simply setting one pointer to NULL does
not solve all ownership problems.

------------------------------------------------------------
56. RETURNING DYNAMIC MEMORY FROM FUNCTION
------------------------------------------------------------

Valid:

    int *create_number(void)
    {
        int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);

        if (p != NULL)
        {
            *p = 100;
        }

        return p;
    }

Usage:

    int *p = create_number();

    if (p != NULL)
    {
        printf("%d\n", *p);
        free(p);
    }

The allocated object's lifetime continues after the function
returns until free is called.

------------------------------------------------------------
57. INVALID RETURN OF LOCAL ADDRESS
------------------------------------------------------------

BAD:

    int *create_number(void)
    {
        int x = 100;

        return &x;
    }

Why?

    x

has automatic storage duration and ceases to exist when the
function returns.

The returned pointer is dangling.

------------------------------------------------------------
58. DYNAMIC LINKED LIST NODE
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    struct Node
    {
        int data;
        struct Node *next;
    };

Create:

    struct Node *node =
        malloc(sizeof *node);

Initialize:

    if (node != NULL)
    {
        node->data = 10;
        node->next = NULL;
    }

This is the basic building block of a linked list.

------------------------------------------------------------
59. LINKED LIST ALLOCATION
------------------------------------------------------------

Conceptually:

    head
     |
     v
    +------+------+
    |  10  |  *---|----+
    +------+------+    |
                       v
                    +------+------+
                    |  20  |  *---|----> NULL
                    +------+------+

Each node is dynamically allocated.

Therefore cleanup must eventually free every node.

------------------------------------------------------------
60. CLEANING A LINKED LIST
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    struct Node *current = head;

    while (current != NULL)
    {
        struct Node *next = current->next;

        free(current);

        current = next;
    }

    head = NULL;

Why save next first?

After:

    free(current);

you cannot safely access:

    current->next

because current's object lifetime has ended.

------------------------------------------------------------
61. MEMORY SAFETY
------------------------------------------------------------

Dynamic memory errors include:

    • Buffer overflow
    • Out-of-bounds access
    • Use-after-free
    • Double free
    • Memory leak
    • Invalid free
    • Integer overflow in allocation size
    • Null dereference
    • Dangling pointer
    • Uninitialized memory use

These bugs can cause:

    • Crashes
    • Data corruption
    • Security vulnerabilities
    • Unpredictable behavior

------------------------------------------------------------
62. INVALID free()
------------------------------------------------------------

Only pass a pointer to allocated storage returned by an
appropriate allocation function, or NULL.

BAD:

    int x = 10;

    free(&x);

x was not dynamically allocated.

Result:

    Undefined behavior

------------------------------------------------------------
63. FREEING MIDDLE OF ALLOCATION
------------------------------------------------------------

BAD:

    int *p = malloc(10 * sizeof *p);

    free(p + 3);

The pointer passed to free must be the pointer returned by the
allocation function (or NULL), not an interior pointer.

Correct:

    free(p);

------------------------------------------------------------
64. BUFFER OVERFLOW
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int *p = malloc(5 * sizeof *p);

    if (p != NULL)
    {
        for (int i = 0; i <= 5; i++)
        {
            p[i] = i;
        }
    }

Problem:

Valid indices:

    0
    1
    2
    3
    4

But:

    p[5]

is outside the allocation.

This causes undefined behavior.

Correct:

    i < 5

------------------------------------------------------------
65. OFF-BY-ONE ERROR
------------------------------------------------------------

Wrong:

    for (size_t i = 0; i <= n; i++)

Correct:

    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)

This is one of the most common memory bugs in C.

